    "turn_text": "And again, the shame of it is that this wouldn't even address the budget problems that supposedly that we need to fix. I'm also someone who is somewhat predisposed to take concerns about the deficit seriously. We do have a deficit. Long-term debt is on a worrying trajectory. And I think we should have some sort of serious attempt. to control it in some way. But you know, $5 trillion in tax cuts is not a serious method of doing that. And like I said, I don't know, at some point you just get cynical about it. But one question I have for you is kind of someone in the middle of it, someone who's voting on this stuff and who cares deeply about this. I know when I talk to members of Congress and people who work in their offices, they would virtually always rather be talking about programs like this, about Medicaid, about SNAP, about the Department of Education, then talking about whatever's going on with some dumb culture war battle. But they always get sucked into talking about the culture war stuff rather than this. First, I guess I appreciate you being here for us to talk about this, to spend the time actually talking about programs that affect 100 million Americans. But how do we get the public's attention on this? How do we spend more time communicating like, this is the thing that matters. This is what's going to impact people's lives. And why is the media not screaming from the rooftops about this?",
