  {
    "episode_id": 71080,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71080.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "Well, first, I think we as Democrats should acknowledge that there is a problem. Like, if the Republicans are the party of less government, we should not be the party of more government. We should be the party of better, cheaper, and faster government. And we should acknowledge that there are inefficiencies. There are what the federal government calls improper payments. A few years ago, the government accountability office found that there were about $186 billion worth of improper payments among five programs, the Medicaid and Medicare, the earned income tax credit, pandemic, unemployment assistance, and PPP. And so that's not a small number. That's a number that we have to take seriously. But the key to rooting out in efficiency is not to block a number like $880 billion out of thin air and then make draconian cuts of the program at the cost of diminishing access to healthcare. The solution lies in changing the incentives that govern our healthcare system. I feel like we do have to reach a point where we are rethinking the fee-for-service model that has historically governed the healthcare system. We should structure our healthcare system to reward not the quantity of health services, but the quality of healthcare. We should aim for what has been described as value-based procurement.",
