    "turn_text": "Medicaid has become two programs. It's become health insurance for the lowest income, and it's become long-term care for the elderly and the chronically ill and the disabled. And there's a competition for resources between those two programs. And in New York, we've seen exponential growth in long-term care, and it's been poorly managed. There's a fundamental room for improvement. We have a program known as CDPAP, which enables the elderly and the disabled to choose their own family members or their own friends as caregivers. And the growth in the program has been unsustainable. In the span of a decade, the program went from 12,000 consumers to a quarter of a million. And the cost of the program has risen to $9 billion. And it has no licensing requirements, no oversight, no supervision. So reforming a program like CDPAP would have a real impact in reining and Medicaid costs in a state like New York. So there's certainly room for more efficient management, no question.",
